menu "OpenReader"

    config OPENREADER_OSDP_ADDRESS
        int "OSDP PD address"
        range 0 126
        default 0
        help
            The 7-bit address this PD answers (0x00..0x7E). Must match what
            the ACU polls. The broadcast address 0x7F is always answered in
            addition and is not configurable.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_BAUD
        int "RS-485 baud rate"
        default 9600
        help
            Must match the ACU. OSDP defines 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600,
            115200 and 230400. 9600 is the spec's mandatory default and the
            right choice for first bring-up.

    config OPENREADER_SECURE_CHANNEL
        bool "OSDP Secure Channel (SC1)"
        default y
        help
            Encrypts and authenticates everything on the bus. Without it a
            credential crosses the wire in the clear, and anyone who can
            reach the cable can read a badge going past and replay it.

            Turn this off only for bring-up against a panel that does not
            speak SC, or to watch decoded frames on a bus monitor. A reader
            on a door needs it on. Off, the firmware says CLEAR TEXT on the
            LCD and at every boot, which is the honest thing for it to say.

            AES-128 comes from mbedTLS on the chip's own accelerator and the
            randomness from the hardware RNG; both already ship with
            ESP-IDF, so this costs no external dependency.

    config OPENREADER_SC_INSTALL_MODE
        bool "Start in install mode (accept SCBK-D until keyed)"
        depends on OPENREADER_SECURE_CHANNEL
        default y
        help
            A reader that has never been given a key answers the handshake
            on SCBK-D — the default install key from spec D.4, the constant
            0x30..0x3F that every OSDP implementation knows. That is how a
            panel reaches a factory-fresh reader in order to give it a real
            key with osdp_KEYSET.

            SCBK-D is not a secret and install mode is not security. It is
            the state in which security gets configured, and a reader left
            in it is one anyone on the bus can talk to. The LCD says INSTALL
            rather than SECURE for exactly that reason.

            Once an operational key is stored the reader stops accepting
            SCBK-D, permanently, until the key is physically reset. That is
            what makes being keyed mean something.

            Turning this off produces a reader that will not talk to any
            panel until a key reaches it some other way — there is no other
            way in this firmware — so it is only useful as a deliberate
            brick. Leave it on.

    config OPENREADER_KEY_RESET
        bool "Physical key reset on the BOOT button"
        depends on OPENREADER_SECURE_CHANNEL
        default y
        help
            Holding the BOOT button (GPIO9) while the firmware is running
            erases the stored key and returns the reader to install mode,
            then reboots.

            This is the only way back. osdp_KEYSET is one-way by design: a
            keyed reader refuses SCBK-D, so a lost key or a replaced panel
            would otherwise mean a reader nothing can talk to. Deliberately
            physical — a path back to a published key that could be reached
            over the wire would be the very hole the key exists to close.

            It does not disturb what BOOT already does. The ROM samples that
            pin at reset; this reads it long afterwards. Holding BOOT across
            a reset still enters download mode with the key untouched.

            Turn it off for a reader whose enclosure cannot be trusted to
            keep hands off the board, accepting that a lost key then means
            re-flashing the device.

    config OPENREADER_KEY_RESET_HOLD_MS
        int "How long the button must be held (ms)"
        depends on OPENREADER_KEY_RESET
        range 2000 60000
        default 10000
        help
            Long enough that it cannot be an accident, and that someone
            reaching for RESET and hitting BOOT does not wipe a working
            reader. Not so long that an installer up a ladder concludes it
            is broken and lets go.

            The hold must be continuous: releasing restarts it from zero.
            The LED counts the hold down in amber so there is something to
            watch while it runs.

    config OPENREADER_DISPLAY
        bool "Show the reader face on the onboard LCD"
        default y
        help
            Draws a virtual reader on the 1.47 inch ST7789: a colour disc
            mirroring the WS2812 (the ACU's osdp_LED colour while the link
            is up, the blue breath while it is down), the PD address, the
            link state, and a card panel showing the last UID read.

            The panel is the only thing that must have hardware SPI2 — its
            clock and data are fixed on-board at GPIO7 and GPIO6 and cannot
            be moved. An RC522 on bit-banged SPI runs alongside it happily.

    config OPENREADER_RC522
        bool "External MFRC522 card reader"
        default y
        help
            Reads ISO/IEC 14443-A cards and reports what it finds to the ACU
            as osdp_RAW — the UID, or a PKOC credential where the card
            carries one and PKOC is built. Turn off for a display-only or
            bus-only build.

    choice OPENREADER_RC522_BUS
        prompt "How the RC522 is clocked"
        depends on OPENREADER_RC522
        default OPENREADER_RC522_SOFT_SPI
        help
            The ESP32-C6 has one general-purpose SPI master and an SPI host
            has one set of bus pins. When the LCD is built it owns that
            host, because the panel's pins are fixed and the RC522's are
            ours to choose — so the RC522 is the one that gives way.

            Giving way costs very little. The hardware path clocks at
            5 MHz and the software path at roughly 500 kHz, but a card poll
            is only a few dozen register accesses; at ten polls a second
            the difference is well under a millisecond of CPU per second,
            against an OSDP tick that must not be late by more than a few.

    config OPENREADER_RC522_HW_SPI
        bool "Hardware SPI2 — requires the LCD to be off"
        depends on !OPENREADER_DISPLAY
        help
            SPI2 in hardware, on the RC522's header pins. Only selectable
            with the display disabled, since the two cannot share the host.

    config OPENREADER_RC522_SOFT_SPI
        bool "Bit-banged SPI — runs alongside the LCD"
        help
            Clocks the RC522 in software on the same header pins, leaving
            hardware SPI2 free for the panel. This is what lets the reader
            face and real card reads coexist.

    endchoice


    config OPENREADER_PKOC
        bool "PKOC credentials (Public Key Open Credential)"
        depends on OPENREADER_RC522
        default y
        help
            Reads PKOC 1.1 credentials as well as UIDs. The card holds a
            P-256 key pair in its secure element and its public key is the
            credential; at the door the card signs a nonce this reader
            supplies, the reader verifies it, and only then derives the
            value the ACU is sent.

            That is worth having because a reader holding no secret is a
            reader with nothing to steal, and cloning a card needs the
            private key rather than the bits it broadcasts — unlike a UID,
            which anyone with a phone can copy off a badge in a pocket.

            Needs the AsymCred library as a sibling checkout, or
            ASYMCRED_DIR pointing at one. Turning this off removes the
            dependency entirely and leaves a UID-only reader.

            Cards that have no PKOC applet are unaffected: the reader tries
            PKOC only when the card's SAK says it speaks ISO/IEC 14443-4,
            and falls back to reporting the UID when the applet is absent.

    choice OPENREADER_PKOC_CRED
        prompt "Credential width reported to the ACU"
        depends on OPENREADER_PKOC
        default OPENREADER_PKOC_CRED_256
        help
            All three forms are taken from the X component of the card's
            public key, and all three are defined by the PKOC specification.
            They differ only in how much of it reaches the panel.

            Whatever you pick has to match how the credential was enrolled
            in the access control system. A 75-bit reader and a 256-bit
            enrolment do not meet in the middle; they simply never match.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_CRED_256
        bool "256 bits — the whole X component"
        help
            32 bytes in the osdp_RAW payload. The full credential, with no
            truncation and so no chance of two cards colliding. Needs an ACU
            that accepts a raw bit field this wide; that is the normal case
            for anything with bi-directional communication to the head end,
            and the reason this is the default.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_CRED_75
        bool "75 bits — PSIA's recommendation for legacy panels"
        help
            10 bytes, top five bits zero. What the specification recommends
            where the panel cannot carry the full key.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_CRED_64
        bool "64 bits — the minimum the specification defines"
        help
            8 bytes. Only for a panel that cannot take 75.

    endchoice

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE
        bool "Verify the card's signature before reporting anything"
        depends on OPENREADER_PKOC
        default y
        help
            Leave this on for any reader on a door.

            With it off the reader reports whatever public key a card
            offers, without checking the card can prove it owns the matching
            private key. That is a value the card claimed — replayable by
            anyone who has watched one exchange — and it makes a PKOC
            credential worth exactly as much as a UID.

            The only honest uses are enrolment, where reading a key off a
            new card is the entire point, and bench diagnostics. The
            firmware logs a warning at every boot while it is off.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_UID_FALLBACK
        bool "Report the UID when a card has no PKOC applet"
        depends on OPENREADER_PKOC
        default y
        help
            On by default so a mixed population of badges keeps working: a
            card with no PKOC applet is reported by UID exactly as before.

            Turn it off for a PKOC-only door. Then a card that cannot
            authenticate produces nothing at all, which is the stricter and
            more defensible position — a UID is not evidence of anything,
            and a panel that accepts both has the weaker of the two as its
            real security.

            Either way, a card whose signature fails to verify is never
            reported by UID. That is not a fallback, it is a failed proof.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_SITE_ID
        string "PKOC site key identifier (32 hex characters)"
        depends on OPENREADER_PKOC
        default "00000000000000000000000000000000"
        help
            The first half of the 32-byte reader identifier sent in
            AUTHENTICATE. 16 bytes, written as hex with no separators.

            All zeros means this reader has not been assigned a site, which
            is the honest thing for a self-built reader to say. The card is
            not required to act on it — nothing in the exchange depends on
            it — so fill it in when your access control system issues you
            one, and leave it alone until then. A malformed value is
            reported at boot and sent as zeros.

    config OPENREADER_PKOC_LOCATION_ID
        string "PKOC reader location identifier (32 hex characters)"
        depends on OPENREADER_PKOC
        default "00000000000000000000000000000000"
        help
            The second half of the reader identifier: which door this is.
            Same format and the same rules as the site identifier above.

    config OPENREADER_BUZZER
        bool "Audible output — active sounder on GPIO5"
        default n
        help
            Drives an active buzzer from osdp_BUZ. "Active" means the part
            makes its own tone, so the pin is simply on or off; the library
            resolves the command's on/off/count pattern and hands the reader
            the edges.

            Default off, and deliberately so: enabling this also makes
            osdp_CAP report an audible output, and claiming a sounder that
            is not fitted is a lie the ACU may act on — it will command
            beeps that never happen and have no way to find out. Turn it on
            when the part is actually on the header.

    config OPENREADER_BUZZER_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "The sounder sounds when the pin is LOW"
        depends on OPENREADER_BUZZER
        default n
        help
            Cheap three-pin modules disagree about polarity. If yours
            shrieks from boot and goes quiet only when the ACU commands a
            beep, it is active low — set this.

    config OPENREADER_TAMPER
        bool "Enclosure tamper switch on GPIO4"
        default n
        help
            Reads a tamper switch and reports it to the ACU in the tamper
            byte of osdp_LSTATR — both when the ACU asks with osdp_LSTAT and
            unsolicited, on the next poll after the switch changes.

            Wire a normally-open switch between GPIO4 and ground. The
            internal pull-up supplies the other level, so the closed
            enclosure leaves the pin high and opening it closes the switch
            and pulls the pin low.

            GPIO4 is the microSD slot's chip select on this carrier, so this
            gives the card slot up. Nothing in the firmware uses it. GPIO4 is
            also an SDIO clock-edge strapping pin, which is harmless here
            because this firmware never uses SDIO — see board.h.

            Default off. Unlike the buzzer this claims no capability the ACU
            could act on, so leaving it on with no switch fitted is not a lie
            to the head end — and with this polarity the pull-up reads an
            unconnected pin as normal rather than as a permanent tamper. It
            is off because an input nothing drives is worth nothing, not
            because it does damage.

    config OPENREADER_TAMPER_NORMALLY_CLOSED
        bool "The switch OPENS when tampered (supervised loop)"
        depends on OPENREADER_TAMPER
        default n
        help
            For a normally-closed switch — one the enclosure lid holds shut
            — instead of the normally-open default. Set this if the reader
            reports tamper with the lid shut and normal with it open.

            It is the more secure wiring if you have the choice. The lid
            holds the pin low, opening it lets the pull-up take the pin
            high, and so does a cut or lifted wire: sabotaging the loop
            reports tamper rather than silence. The normally-open default
            cannot tell a quiet, intact loop apart from a snipped one.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_TX_GPIO
        int "RS-485 UART TX GPIO (to the transceiver's RXD)"
        default 0
        help
            Which pin drives the SH-U12's RXD input.

            The default (GPIO0, header GP0) is a free header GPIO rather
            than the pins silkscreened TX/RX. GPIO18/19 are not usable: 19
            is the RC522's MISO (board.h) and the build rejects the clash. Those labelled pins are
            GPIO16/17 — UART0's default mapping — and they work fine here,
            with one caveat: the ROM bootloader transmits its banner on
            GPIO16 at every reset, so on a live multi-drop bus that chatter
            lands on the wire before your firmware runs.

            Waveshare has shipped more than one revision of this carrier and
            their published documentation does not enumerate the header, so
            trust your board's silkscreen over any default here.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_RX_GPIO
        int "RS-485 UART RX GPIO (from the transceiver's TXD, via the divider)"
        default 1
        help
            Which pin reads the SH-U12's TXD output. Default GPIO1 (header
            GP1).

            Remember this pin must never see the transceiver's raw 5 V — it
            reaches the ESP32-C6 through the resistor divider. See
            docs/HARDWARE.md.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_ECHO_SUPPRESS
        bool "Discard local echo of transmitted bytes"
        default n
        help
            The MAX13487 on the DSD TECH SH-U12 handles direction
            automatically, with no DE/RE pin to toggle. Some auto-direction
            transceivers leave the receiver enabled while driving, so every
            byte the PD transmits comes straight back in on RX. The OSDP
            stream decoder would then have to chew through the PD's own
            replies looking for a frame.

            Enable this and the transport drops exactly as many inbound
            bytes as it just transmitted.

            LEAVE THIS OFF until you have measured your board — see
            docs/HARDWARE.md, "Does your transceiver echo?". If the board
            does not echo and this is on, it will eat the first bytes of the
            ACU's next command and the PD will never answer a poll.

            Measured on the reference build: the DSD TECH SH-U12 does echo,
            and the reader works against a real panel with this left off.
            The stream decoder recognises the PD's own replies as replies
            and drops them, so an echo costs buffer churn and nothing else.
            You do not need this unless you want the churn gone.

    config OPENREADER_CARD_REPEAT_MS
        int "Ignore the same card for this many milliseconds"
        default 2000
        help
            A card left on the antenna is re-read several times a second.
            Reporting each read would flood the ACU with duplicate osdp_RAW
            replies, so an identical UID inside this window is suppressed.

    config OPENREADER_PDID_SERIAL
        hex "PD serial number reported in osdp_PDID"
        default 0x00000001
        help
            Reported in the osdp_PDID reply. Give each unit on a bus a
            distinct value; some ACUs use it to tell devices apart.

endmenu
